<https://api.cloud.ai4eosc.eu/v1/catalog/modules/conus-classification/metadata> a it6:MachineLearningModel ;
    it6:fineTunedFrom <https://github.com/ai4os-hub/ai4os-image-classification-tf> ;
    it6:hasBibliographicReference <http://digital.csic.es/handle/10261/194548> ;
    it6:hasMachineLearningLibrary <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/library/Keras>,
        <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/library/TensorFlow> ;
    it6:hasRepository <https://github.com/ai4os-hub/conus-classification>,
        <https://hub.docker.com/r/ai4oshub/conus-classification> ;
    it6:hasTaskType <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/tasktype/Classification>,
        <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/tasktype/Computer_Vision> ;
    it6:shortDescription "Classify conus images among 70 species."@en ;
    it6:trainedOn <http://www.mncn.csic.es/> ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:description """Citizen science has become a powerful force for scientific inquiry, providing researchers with access to a vast array of data points while connecting non scientists to the real process of science.
This citizen-researcher relationship creates a very interesting synergy, allowing for the creation, execution, and analysis of research projects.

With this in mind, a Convolutional Neural Network has been trained to identify conus marine snails at species level [1] in collaboration with the [Natural Science Museum of Madrid](http://www.mncn.csic.es/).
The taxonomy of these snails has changed significantly several times during recent years and the introduction of Deep Learning techniques allowing to classify them is a very valuable tool for the experts.

This Docker container contains a trained Convolutional Neural network optimized for conus identification using images. The architecture used is an Xception [2] network using Keras on top of Tensorflow.
The PREDICT method expects an RGB image as input (or the url of an RGB image) and will return a JSON with the top 5 predictions.
The training dataset has been provided by the [Natural Science Museum of Madrid](http://www.mncn.csic.es/) and it consists on a dataset containing images of 68 species of conus covering three different regions: the Panamic region; the South African region; and the Western Atlantic and Mediterranean region.

<img class='fit', src='https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ai4os-hub/conus-classification/main/images/conus.png'/> 

This service is based in the [Image Classification with Tensorflow](./deep-oc-image-classification-tensorflow.html) model.

**References**
1. Puillandre, N.; Duda, T.F.; Meyer, C.; Olivera, B.M.; Bouchet, P. (2014). [One, four or 100 genera? A new classification of the cone snails](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4541476/). Journal of Molluscan Studies. 81 (1): 1-23.
2. Chollet, Francois. [Xception: Deep learning with depthwise separable convolutions](https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.02357) arXiv preprint (2017): 1610-02357."""@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "conus-classification" ;
    dcterms:license <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/licence/MIT> ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-11-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:title "Conus species classifier"@en ;
    dcterms:type <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/category/AI4_inference>,
        <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/category/AI4_pre_trained> ;
    dcat:keyword "deep learning"@en,
        "image classification"@en,
        "vo.imagine-ai.eu"@en .

<https://api.cloud.ai4eosc.eu/v1/catalog/modules/deep-species-detection/metadata> a it6:MachineLearningModel ;
    it6:hasMachineLearningLibrary <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/library/PyTorch> ;
    it6:hasRepository <https://github.com/ai4os-hub/deep-species-detection>,
        <https://hub.docker.com/r/ai4oshub/deep-species-detection> ;
    it6:hasTaskType <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/tasktype/Classification>,
        <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/tasktype/Computer_Vision>,
        <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/tasktype/Other> ;
    it6:shortDescription "Deep Sea Species Detection"@en ;
    it6:trainedOn <https://doi.org/10.17882/101899> ;
    dcterms:created "2024-08-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:description """Provided by Ifremer, and iMagine.

This module helps users that want to train a deep learning visual identification algorithm (Yolov8) with citizen science data.
It was developed with the intent to clean and train automatically on Deep Sea Spy data (citizen science project).

The cleaning is done in 2 steps: 
- converting the bounding boxes in a regular format (optional). In Deep Sea Spy, they come in 3 different formats (points, lines and polygons).
- unify bounding boxes, using the redundancy of the information as a confirmation for the presence of an individual. 
There also are other data manipulation/visualisation tools provided in this module to help scientists with the data analysis.

The user can:
- Clean its own dataset using either the Deepas API, the Notebook.ipynb or the pipeline_txt.py file.
- Train with its own data from nextcloud (training is automatic in pipeline_txt.py)
- Use the existing model trained on [Deep Sea Spy data](https://doi.org/10.17882/101899) for inference.

Further information in the README.me file provided within this repository.

This module is mainly based on this already [existing module](https://github.com/ai4os-hub/ai4os-yolov8-torch)

You can find more information on Yolov8 here:
* https://github.com/ultralytics/ultralytics
* https://docs.ultralytics.com

<img class='fit', src='https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ai4os-hub/deep-species-detection/main/data/deepseaspy_annotations_small.png'/>"""@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "deep-species-detection" ;
    dcterms:license <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/licence/AGPL_3_0> ;
    dcterms:modified "2025-05-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:title "Deep Species Detection"@en ;
    dcterms:type <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/category/AI4_inference>,
        <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/category/AI4_pre_trained>,
        <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/category/AI4_trainable> ;
    dcat:keyword "citizen science"@en,
        "data preparation"@en,
        "deep learning"@en,
        "deep sea spy"@en,
        "object detection"@en,
        "vo.imagine-ai.eu"@en .

<https://api.cloud.ai4eosc.eu/v1/catalog/modules/diamorph-classification/metadata> a it6:MachineLearningModel ;
    it6:hasMachineLearningLibrary <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/library/PyTorch> ;
    it6:hasRepository <https://github.com/ai4os-hub/diamorph-classification>,
        <https://hub.docker.com/r/ai4oshub/diamorph-classification> ;
    it6:hasTaskType <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/tasktype/Computer_Vision> ;
    it6:shortDescription "Diatoms classification at the species level using Yolov8-cls of Ultralytics."@en ;
    dcterms:created "2025-07-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:description """diamorph_classification is an application using the DEEPaaS API.

It is designed to classify individual diatoms at the species level. 

The provided image is expected to be 512x512 at a scale of 0.1 µm/pixel .

This preprocessing can be performed using the `square-pad.py` script provided
on the github repository. In any case, in the current version of deployed application,
you **must** perform this pre-preprocessing on your side.

Also, in the currently deployed version of the application, you must adapt the parameters on the gradio interface. The image size must be set to 512.

It is based on a pre-trained yolov8-cls that has been finetuned on manually labeled data.

This application only provides the inference endpoint.

<img class='fit', src='https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ai4os-hub/diamorph-classification/main/data/val_batch.jpg'/>

**History and contributors**

The project was initiated by the PhD of Aishwarya Venkataramanan using YoloV5 working on both detection and classification [1], cosupervized by Cédric Pradalier and Martin Laviale. Aisharya developed a hierarchical classification system. Thanks to the support of PNRIA (Programme National de Recherche en Intelligence Artificielle) and the work of Cyril Regan, the code base was improved [2]. The project was finally ported
to the iMagine platform with Yolov8 by Jeremy Fix. This ported code does not yet support hierarchical classification.

The database used to train the Yolov8 is made of Atlas images of single individuals. After excluding classes with fewer than 10 images, the datasets comprised 108 classes at the genus level (ranging from 11 to 4527 images/class) and 548 classes (ranging from 11 to 395 images/class) at the species level.

**References**

1. Aishwarya Venkataramanan. [Automatic identification of diatoms using deep learning to improve ecological diagnosis of aquatic environments](https://theses.hal.science/tel-04643505). Université de Lorraine, 2023. English.
2. [https://www.ins2i.cnrs.fr/fr/diamorph-identification-automatique-des-diatomees-par-apprentissage-profond-partir-dimages](https://www.ins2i.cnrs.fr/fr/diamorph-identification-automatique-des-diatomees-par-apprentissage-profond-partir-dimages)"""@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "diamorph-classification" ;
    dcterms:license <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/licence/AGPL_3_0> ;
    dcterms:modified "2025-10-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:title "Diatom classification at the species level"@en ;
    dcterms:type <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/category/AI4_inference> ;
    dcat:keyword "deep learning"@en,
        "diatoms classification"@en,
        "vo.imagine-ai.eu"@en .

<https://api.cloud.ai4eosc.eu/v1/catalog/modules/diamorph-detection/metadata> a it6:MachineLearningModel ;
    it6:hasMachineLearningLibrary <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/library/PyTorch> ;
    it6:hasRepository <https://github.com/ai4os-hub/diamorph-detection>,
        <https://hub.docker.com/r/ai4oshub/diamorph-detection> ;
    it6:hasTaskType <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/tasktype/Computer_Vision> ;
    it6:shortDescription "Diatoms detection with oriented bounding boxes."@en ;
    dcterms:created "2025-03-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:description """diamorph_detection is an application using the DEEPaaS API.

It is designed to predict oriented bounding boxes around diatoms. 
It is based on a pre-trained yolov8 that has been finetuned on manually
labeled data.

This application only provides the inference endpoint.


<img class='fit', src='https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ai4os-hub/diamorph-detection/main/data/val_batch.jpg'/>

**History and contributors**

The project was initiated by the PhD of Aishwarya Venkataramanan using YoloV5 working on both detection and classification [1], cosupervized by Cédric Pradalier and Martin Laviale. At that time
it was using axis aligned bounding boxes. Later, thanks to the support of PNRIA (Programme National de Recherche en Intelligence Artificielle) and the work
of Cyril Regan, the code base was improved, the project was ported to Yolov5 with oriented bounding boxes and a web frontend added [2]. The project was finally ported
to the iMagine platform with Yolov8 by Jeremy Fix.

The database used to train the Yolov8 is Cleurie/Orne, 2867 images, and 9822 instances annotated by Corentin Galinier and Martin Laviale. The running model is a pretrained Yolov8m-obb finetuned on the Cleurie/Orne dataset.

**References**

1. Aishwarya Venkataramanan. [Automatic identification of diatoms using deep learning to improve ecological diagnosis of aquatic environments](https://theses.hal.science/tel-04643505). Université de Lorraine, 2023. English.
2. [https://www.ins2i.cnrs.fr/fr/diamorph-identification-automatique-des-diatomees-par-apprentissage-profond-partir-dimages](https://www.ins2i.cnrs.fr/fr/diamorph-identification-automatique-des-diatomees-par-apprentissage-profond-partir-dimages)"""@en ;
    dcterms:identifier "diamorph-detection" ;
    dcterms:license <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/licence/AGPL_3_0> ;
    dcterms:modified "2025-10-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:title "Diatom detection with oriented bounding boxes"@en ;
    dcterms:type <http://w3id.org/ai4os/vocabulary/category/AI4_inference> ;
    dcat:keyword "deep learning"@en,
        "diatoms detection"@en,
